  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1251 AM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nOvernight into Tonight\n\nA surface low will track southeast from southwest Minnesota \ninto southeast Iowa today and into northern Illinois tonight. \nThe initial band of rain will be northwest to southeast from \nsouthwest Minnesota into southeast Minnesota and northeast Iowa.\nAs low pressure gradually deepens this afternoon and tonight, a\ndeformation band will develop over southern Minnesota and \nnorthern Iowa. As this occurs, the band will become more \norientated west to east. Where this occurs, the longevity of the\nrainfall will increase. \n\nPrecipitable water values climb into the 1.75 to 2.4 inch range\nacross southeast Minnesota and northeast Iowa, and from 1.2 to \n1.75 inches this morning. These values then decrease into the \n1.5 to 2 inch range this afternoon and into the 1 to 2 inch \nrange tonight. The highest precipitable water values for this \nafternoon and evening will be south of Interstate 90. \n\nA mesoscale convective complex currently over western Iowa will\ncontinue to move east southeast overnight and this morning. As \nit does, it will keep most of the instability well south of the \nforecast area. There might be some 0-1 km mixed-layer CAPES up \nto 250 J/kg across Fayette and Clayton counties. \n\nThe 31.00z HREF has a mean 24-hour rainfall totals of 1 to 3 \ninches across southeast Minnesota, northeast Iowa, and southwest\nWisconsin. The 24-hour localized probability-matched mean has \ngenerally a 4 to 10 inch along the Buffalo ridge in Minnesota. \nThere is even a very small area in the 10-15 inch range. This \nmakes sense because you have an overlap of the instability and \nhigh precipitable water values (2-2.5 inches) in that area. \nFurther southeast into southeast Minnesota, northeast Iowa, and \nsouthwest Wisconsin there is a narrow potential of 3 to 5 \ninches. The question is where this might occur. With the global \nmodels having a tendency of being too far north with their QPF, \nthere is concern that the models might be too far north and this\nband might be further south closer to the better instability. \nWe have already seen a slight south in the CAMs this evening. \nEarlier in the evening, they were either along or north of \nInterstate 90. They are now more south of Interstate 90. Will \nthere be more of southern shift as we progress through the \novernight and into Friday morning?\n\nHigh temperatures today will range from the upper 60s to \nmid-70s. Low temperatures will be around 60 for tonight.\n\nSaturday\n\nThe surface low will slowly move southeast across northern \nIllinois. There is general consensus in the global models and \ntheir ensemble members for a deformation band to orientate \nitself from northeast to southwest just southeast of the \nsouthwest and central Wisconsin. They will likely see some more \nrain, but it would likely be on the light side (up to a tenth of\nan inch). High temperatures will range from the mid-70s \n(southwest and central Wisconsin to around 80 elsewhere.\n\nSaturday Night into Monday\n\nHigh pressure will keep the area dry from Saturday night into \nMonday. High temperatures will range from around 80 to the \nmid-80s. Low temperatures will range from the lower 50s to lower\n60s.\n\nMonday Night into Wednesday\n\nA cold front will move into the area on Monday night and then \nlinger across the area into Wednesday. While the 0-1 km mixed- \nlayer CAPES will occasionally climb up to 1000 J/kg at times, \nboth the 0-3 and 0- 6 km shear remains weak. As a result not \nseeing any organized severe weather at this time. High \ntemperatures will range from the mid-70s to mid-80s. Low \ntemperatures will range from the mid-50s to mid-60s.",
